findings.body:
Removes the origin allowlist and always sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to the request’s `Origin` (or `*`) while still sending `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`.

**Failure mode:** a malicious page at `https://evil.example` sends a credentialed XHR/fetch to this API. The response includes `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://evil.example` and `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`, so the browser exposes the response body (session cookies, bearer cookies, etc.) to the attacker’s script. That is full cross-site data theft for any cookie-authenticated endpoint behind this middleware.

Native mobile clients do not use browser CORS; webviews that do should get specific allowed origins added to the set, not a wildcard/reflect-all policy.

```suggestion
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return { Vary: "Origin" };
```

Restore `const ALLOWED = new Set([...])` (including any real mobile-webview origins) above the function.
